To fix the overlapping content issue on the Status screen, we need to apply the same Z-index and Background fix to your modal content that you implemented in the Authorization page.
The issue occurs because the DialogContent or the Box containing your workflow does not have an explicit background-color or z-index to block the scrolling text.
Here is the change needed in **VoucherPostingStatus.js**:
### 1. Update the Modal Container Style
Find your DialogContent block for the workflow details. We need to ensure the container housing the content has a solid background color to mask the text scrolling beneath the header.
**Find this block in VoucherPostingStatus.js:**
```javascript
          <DialogContent dividers sx={{ p: 0, backgroundColor: "#f8fafc", height: '75vh', display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'column' }}>
            <Box sx={{ display: "flex", flexDirection: "column", flexGrow: 1, overflowY: "auto" }}>

```
**Replace it with this:**
```javascript
          <DialogContent dividers sx={{ p: 0, backgroundColor: "#f8fafc", height: '75vh', display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'column' }}>
            {/* FIX: Added z-index and background color to prevent text bleed-through */}
            <Box sx={{ 
              display: "flex", 
              flexDirection: "column", 
              flexGrow: 1, 
              overflowY: "auto",
              backgroundColor: "white", 
              position: "relative",
              zIndex: 1 
            }}>

```
### 2. Apply Sticky Headers to the Content Sections
Since you mentioned the text is visible "out of the header," your inner header cards also need to be pinned correctly so they don't scroll transparently.
**Find your "HEADER CARDS" section and ensure it has a solid background:**
```javascript
              {/* ---  HEADER CARDS --- */}
              <Box sx={{ 
                  p: 4, 
                  backgroundColor: "white", // Must be solid white
                  borderBottom: "1px solid", 
                  borderColor: "divider",
                  position: "sticky",       // Pins it to the top
                  top: 0, 
                  zIndex: 10                // Ensures it stays above scrolling content
              }}>

```
### Why this fixes the problem:
 1. **backgroundColor: "white"**: By setting the background to white on the header box, you create an opaque "curtain." As the batch details scroll up, they will pass *behind* this white background instead of appearing on top of it.
 2. **position: sticky and zIndex: 10**: This mimics the exact behavior you implemented in the Authorization page. It ensures the header remains fixed at the top while the content flows underneath it, maintaining the professional look you expect.
After applying these two changes, the scrolling text will disappear exactly at the edge of the header, just like it does on your Authorization page.
